<BaseLayout title="Zhangjiajie Glass Bridge | Grand Canyon Guide (2026)" description="Complete guide to Zhangjiajie Glass Bridge: tickets, opening hours, how to get there, what to expect, and how to book the best time slot." author="Zhangjiajie Guide Team" datePublished="July 2026">
<slot name="head"><script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)}></script></slot>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 md:px-6 pb-20 md:pb-28">
  <div class="inline-flex items-center rounded-full border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 px-3 py-1 text-xs font-medium text-forest-700 mb-6">Glass Bridge</div>
  <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-stone-900 leading-tight">Zhangjiajie Glass Bridge</h1>
  <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">The first step onto the glass is the hardest. After that it is just a really long bridge with an extraordinary view through the floor. 430 meters long, 300 meters high, and completely transparent underfoot. The trick is timing — go early enough and you can stand still, look down, and actually feel the height before the crowd sweeps you along.</p>
  <nav class="mt-6 flex items-center gap-2 text-sm text-stone-400" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/" class="hover:text-forest-700 transition-colors">Home</a><svg width="12" height="12" viewBox="0 0 16 16" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M6 3l5 5-5 5"/></svg><span class="text-stone-600">Glass Bridge</span></nav>

  

  <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
    <picture>
      <source srcset="/banner-glassbridge.webp" type="image/webp" />
      <img
        src="/banner-glassbridge.jpg"
        alt="Zhangjiajie Glass Bridge suspended above Grand Canyon"
        class="w-full aspect-[21/9] object-cover"
        width="1200"
        height="514"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </picture>
  </div>
    <div class="mt-12 space-y-12">
    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">Practical Information</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 overflow-hidden"><table class="w-full text-sm"><tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100"><tr><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600 font-medium w-32">Location</td><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600">Zhangjiajie Grand Canyon, about 60 km from Wulingyuan</td></tr><tr><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600 font-medium">Ticket Price</td><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600">CNY 128 (bridge only). Combo with canyon hike + boat: CNY 219</td></tr><tr><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600 font-medium">Hours</td><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600">8:00 AM – 5:00 PM (last entry 4:00 PM)</td></tr><tr><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600 font-medium">Time Slots</td><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600">8:00, 9:00, 10:00, 11:00, 12:30, 13:30, 14:30 — book online at least a day ahead</td></tr><tr><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600 font-medium">Restrictions</td><td class="px-5 py-3 text-stone-600">No selfie sticks, no drones, no large bags (free lockers available), no high heels</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
        <picture>
          <source srcset="/feature-3.webp" type="image/webp" />
          <img
            src="/feature-3.jpg"
            alt="Zhangjiajie Glass Bridge spanning the Grand Canyon with sandstone cliffs on both sides"
            class="w-full aspect-[16/9] object-cover"
            width="800"
            height="600"
            loading="lazy"
            decoding="async"
          />
        </picture>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">What It Feels Like</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">The bridge itself is a flat walkway with glass panels set into the floor. The glass is 6 cm thick — three layers laminated together — and completely transparent. You see the canyon floor 300 meters below through your feet. Walking onto it the first time triggers a genuine adrenaline response, even for people who are not afraid of heights. I have no fear of heights, and my first step onto the glass still made me hesitate.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">We visited in April 2026 with an 8 AM time slot. By 8:30 the bridge was busy but not crowded; by 10 AM it was shoulder-to-shoulder. The experience is dramatically better with fewer people — you can stop mid-span, look down, and actually feel the height rather than being swept along by the crowd. At its peak, the bridge was recognized as the world's longest and highest glass-bottom bridge by Guinness World Records. It holds the record because of the engineering: a glass surface that spans 430 meters without intermediate supports.</p>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-amber-200 bg-amber-50 p-5 text-sm text-amber-800">
        <strong>Important:</strong> Bring your passport. Like all Zhangjiajie attractions, the ticket is linked to your passport number. No passport = no entry. Camera phones are fine; professional cameras with large lenses may be restricted on busy days.
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">After the Bridge: Canyon Hike</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">After crossing the bridge, you have two options. Both end at a boat dock that takes you to the exit. Our full <a href="/itinerary" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">3-day itinerary</a> includes the Glass Bridge on Day 3 and shows how the timing fits with the rest of your trip.</p>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-800 text-lg mb-2">Option A: Canyon Hike (2-2.5 hours)</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500">Descend into the Grand Canyon via a stone staircase embedded in the cliff. The trail follows a stream through narrow gorges, past several waterfalls, and ends at a boat dock deep in the valley. The hike is mostly downhill but involves about 1,000 steps total — your knees will feel it the next day. At the end, a short boat ride (included in the combo ticket at CNY 219) takes you across the lake to the exit. Worth doing if you have the energy and want to see the canyon from the bottom looking up.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-800 text-lg mb-2">Option B: Slide + Boat (1 hour)</h3>
          <p class="text-sm text-stone-500">A 600-meter transparent tube slide (CNY 30) spirals down from the bridge exit to the canyon floor. It is genuinely fun and surprisingly fast — you can control your speed with your feet. From the slide exit, a 10-minute walk leads to the same boat dock. Less scenic than the full hike, but much easier on the knees. We did the slide in April and would choose it again unless you specifically want the canyon walk. The combo of bridge + slide makes for a memorable morning.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">How to Get There</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">The Grand Canyon entrance is about 60 km from Wulingyuan. From the Wulingyuan bus station, direct minibuses marked "大峡谷" (Grand Canyon) run every 30 minutes from 7 AM. The ride costs CNY 25 and takes about 40 minutes along a winding road through farmland and low hills.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">From Zhangjiajie city center, take any bus to Wulingyuan first (CNY 12, 40 minutes), then transfer to the Grand Canyon minibus. Total journey about 1.5 hours. A taxi from Wulingyuan costs CNY 80-100 one-way — we took a taxi on our first visit and the bus on our second. The bus is fine; the taxi saves about 15 minutes. For detailed transport options, see our <a href="/how-to-get-there" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-700 underline">how to get there guide</a>.</p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-stone-900 mb-4">Is It Worth It?</h2>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed mb-4">CNY 128 for 15 minutes of walking and potentially 30 minutes of queuing — it is the most expensive single attraction in the park, and you should know what you are paying for. The glass bridge experience itself is spectacular but brief. Literally 10-15 minutes to walk across at a normal pace. The photo opportunities are unique. The adrenaline hit is real. And if you have seen the photos of people lying on the glass or posing for Instagram, you will find those spots easily.</p>
      <p class="text-stone-500 leading-relaxed">Our honest take: it is worth doing with the 8 AM slot and the combo ticket (CNY 219 including the canyon hike and boat). The bridge alone feels thin for the price. Bridge + canyon descent + boat ride is a solid half-day excursion. Without the canyon portion, we would rank it below Avatar Mountain and Tianzi Mountain on the priority list. With the canyon and slide, it is one of the most fun mornings in the park.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
